WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Cross-Site Request Forgery, or CSRF, is a browser-session risk involving unwanted state-changing actions.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.600
The key idea is not that a user is unknown to the application.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.860
The user may already be authenticated.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.720
The issue is that an authenticated browser request is not always an intended request from the user.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.000
CSRF matters most when a browser automatically includes existing credentials, especially cookies.

6
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:32.300
A server-rendered ASP.NET Core application may rely on authentication cookies for a signed-in session.

7
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:44.160
When the browser sends those cookies according to cookie rules, the server can identify the user, but it still needs a way to know whether a protected state-changing action came from the intended application interaction.

8
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.880
State-changing actions deserve special attention.

9
00:00:47.980 --> 00:01:00.140
Creating, modifying, deleting, submitting, approving, purchasing, transferring, configuring, or otherwise changing server-side state has different risk than reading a public page.

10
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:07.520
A request that changes data should meet stronger expectations than a request that only reads a low-risk resource.

11
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.680
The defensive idea is user intent.

12
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.060
Authentication answers who the browser session belongs to.

13
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.920
Authorization answers whether that user may perform the action.

14
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:25.380
CSRF prevention adds a signal that the protected request came through the intended application flow.

15
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.000
Those controls are related, but they answer different questions.

16
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.040
In ASP.NET Core, CSRF prevention is part of normal application design.

17
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:47.940
Teams should review state-changing endpoints, cookie behavior, form conventions, JavaScript clients, SameSite settings, CORS assumptions, Data Protection configuration, and logging.

18
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.320
The goal is not a fear-based checklist.

19
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.940
The goal is predictable request handling for browser-based workflows.
